WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:06.180
MANAGEMENT CONSULTANTS:
We all know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:25.200
that one consultant who travels the&nbsp;
world, works with the biggest companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:31.860
and gets a massive salary. They usually work at&nbsp;
McKinsey and Co, Boston Consulting Group, or some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:39.060
other big name firm. But, what exactly do these&nbsp;
people actually accomplish? Well, theoretically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:46.320
they’re supposed to bring in an expert perspective&nbsp;
in a specific field or industry. But, most of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:52.860
time, they’re completely useless. Now, before I&nbsp;
get clobbered in the comments, let me specify that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:58.320
I’m not talking about technical consultants.&nbsp;
Engineering consultants, legal consultants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:03.900
security consultants, and IT consultants can&nbsp;
be extraordinarily valuable given that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:09.900
truly possess expertise in a certain area. This&nbsp;
expertise is often something that many companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:16.260
lack internally, so they have to outsource certain&nbsp;
work to these external firms. But, there’s another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:21.480
breed of consultants, management consultants,&nbsp;
who are virtually useless. For starters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:27.840
the average consultant at these big name firms&nbsp;
are between 20 and 30 years old. And over ⅓ of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:34.020
them have only been at the company for a mere 1 to&nbsp;
2 years. So, how could a 25 year old with 2 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:40.560
of experience possibly have any idea regarding how&nbsp;
Apple or Microsoft or even small company should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:46.140
manage their business. These individuals have no&nbsp;
management experience whatsoever, and everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:52.380
they know is theoretical and from a textbook. And&nbsp;
I think we can all agree that management isn’t a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:57.360
skill that you learn from a book or case studies.&nbsp;
Great management is something you learn from years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:03.300
of leadership, management, and actually doing the&nbsp;
job that your managing. The average management&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:09.900
consultant lacks all of these traits, yet they&nbsp;
often achieve 6 figures out of college and over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:15.360
$200,000 by the time they’re 30. Now, you might&nbsp;
be saying that these junior consultants are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:20.400
usually led by senior consultants who do know&nbsp;
what they’re doing. But, to that I would argue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:25.560
that most companies have internal staff that are&nbsp;
more knowledgeable than senior consultants. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:31.380
example, an internal program manager who has 20&nbsp;
years of experience across 4 or 5 big companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:36.420
in a specific domain will likely outperform&nbsp;
a senior management consultant with broad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:42.480
experience everyday of the week. Hearing all this,&nbsp;
you’re probably wondering why in the world do top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:49.320
consulting firms pull tens of billions if they’re&nbsp;
so useless. Well, one things for sure, it’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:55.020
for their management expertise. So, here’s why&nbsp;
companies actually hire management consultants.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:03.660
BREAKING BARRIERS:
One of the most common reasons that companies hire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:09.736
consultants are to break through various barriers,&nbsp;
both internally and externally. If you’re a high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.736 --> 00:03:14.820
ranking executive at a $10 plus billion company,&nbsp;
you’re probably going to have handle a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:19.860
politics. This includes things like backstabbing&nbsp;
and blackmailing colleagues, trash talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:25.500
competent employees, and intentionally withholding&nbsp;
crucial information. Why do people resort to such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:31.980
measures you ask? Well, it usually has to do with&nbsp;
jealousy, ego, and getting revenge. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:38.280
Betty who works across from you and is obviously&nbsp;
less productive may get that promotion before you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:44.460
do. And suddenly, you have to report to Betty.&nbsp;
From your perspective, Betty doesn’t deserve that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:49.860
title, and you’re honestly pretty salty that you&nbsp;
didn’t get the promotion yourself. So, the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:56.280
time that Betty tells you do something, you may be&nbsp;
inclined to be unresponsive, push back, or put up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:02.100
unnecessary roadblocks. Usually, you can’t just&nbsp;
fire such employees because you’d end up laying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:07.380
off you’re entire workforce. Not to mention, in&nbsp;
most cases, such employees aren’t necessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:13.260
incompetent or not hard working. They just need&nbsp;
someone to get through their hardhead and get them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:18.360
do what’s right for the business. Enter management&nbsp;
consultants. One of the biggest roles of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:24.180
management consultants is to identify painpoints&nbsp;
and offer solutions, but they wont actually make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:30.180
decisions for you. This is actually a big meme&nbsp;
within the industry. People often criticize that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:36.300
consultants just tell you what you already know,&nbsp;
and this is very much true. But, the thing to note&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:42.780
is that they do it in a much more credible manner.&nbsp;
For example, instead of just saying that fixing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:47.100
supply chain bottleneck will save the company&nbsp;
money, they’ll actually get the numbers, put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:53.040
together graphs, and create a solid presentation.&nbsp;
Virtually 100% of the time, they’re gonna come to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:59.520
the same conclusion as Betty, but as an employee,&nbsp;
it’s much harder to say no to a 100 page slide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:04.380
deck filled with charts and graphs than it is to&nbsp;
say no to Betty’s seemingly subjective analysis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:09.600
Ideally, instead of paying consulting firms an&nbsp;
arm and a leg to facilitate healthy objective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:14.760
communcation between employees, companies should&nbsp;
work on creating a better workplace culture. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:19.560
that’s much easier said than done. Also,&nbsp;
if your company grows large enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:24.780
it’s just a matter of time until there starts to&nbsp;
be a disconnect between the end product and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:30.600
average employee. And at this point, it’s just&nbsp;
a matter of time until you start to see rampant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:36.660
corporate politics. Employees will no longer&nbsp;
be loyal to the product or customer. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:42.660
they become loyal to the salary, the prestige, the&nbsp;
job title, and maybe the performance review, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:48.060
that’s pretty much. Meanwhile, the employees who&nbsp;
do want to make change and produce impact will no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:54.180
longer be interested in working at your company.&nbsp;
This is truly an unfortunate situation, but you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:59.820
as the leader can’t just give in and let employees&nbsp;
do whatever they want. So, you’re often forced to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:04.080
bring in management consultants to breakthrough&nbsp;
the corporate barriers across the organization.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:08.520
UNPOPULAR DECISIONS:&nbsp;

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:14.580
Consultants aren’t just useful at breaking through&nbsp;
corporate politics. They’re also especially useful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:20.040
when it comes to making hard unpopular decisions.&nbsp;
This is particularly true in the current economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:25.920
climate because lots of unpopular choices need to&nbsp;
be made during downturns. For example, this might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:30.900
include shutting down a sector or laying off a&nbsp;
substantial number of employees. And contrary to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:36.960
popular belief, the vast majority of CEOs don’t&nbsp;
want to lay off employees for two reasons. One,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:42.420
they’re actually good people and they feel bad&nbsp;
cutting off so many people’s incomes. Or two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:46.800
they’re cynical and they don’t want the&nbsp;
bad press and reputation associated with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:52.800
laying off so many people. A perfect example&nbsp;
of the latter is Jeff Bezos. Back in November,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:57.360
on the same day that rumors started to float&nbsp;
around that Amazon was gonna do layoffs, Jeff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:02.280
Bezos announced that he would donate all of his&nbsp;
wealth to charity. Seems like an odd coincidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:08.460
to me, but you can be the judge of that. Anyway,&nbsp;
regardless of whether a CEO wants to avoid layoffs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:14.580
for respectable reasons or cynical reasons,&nbsp;
in the end, they want to avoid layoffs. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:20.340
at the same time, they know that deep down, it’s&nbsp;
the right choice for the business. But, how are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:25.740
they gonna convince thousands of managers, tens of&nbsp;
thousands of employees, and millions of investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:32.400
to at least somewhat agree with them? Well, enter&nbsp;
management consultants. It will essentially be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:37.860
consultants jobs to justify the layoffs through&nbsp;
objective means. For example, they might make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:43.500
charts regarding just how much money a particular&nbsp;
sector is losing and why the ROI may not be worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:48.960
it. They may also quantify how much money layoffs&nbsp;
could save the company and how this could put the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:55.140
company in a much better position in case of&nbsp;
a deeper recession. Once McKinsey or BCG puts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.140 --> 00:08:00.060
together all of this data, the CEO can send&nbsp;
out the findings to the various managers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:06.060
directors across the organization followed by a&nbsp;
heartfelt message. They can say something like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:13.200
“I really didn’t want it to come down to this, but&nbsp;
after seeing this 1000 page report from McKinsey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:19.320
I feel that there is no choice but to move forward&nbsp;
with these layoffs.” This report doesn’t just help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:24.660
the executives either. It also helps the various&nbsp;
directors and managers who will directly be laying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:30.240
off team members that they’ve been working with&nbsp;
for years. I mean these guys aren’t stupid. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:35.400
also know that making these cuts is the right&nbsp;
choice. But, given that they personally know the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:40.980
people being laid off, it’s much harder for them&nbsp;
to pull the trigger. They may even fight for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:46.080
certain employee or manager, but the McKinsey&nbsp;
report does wonders when it comes to making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:52.380
this process as smooth as possible. With all that&nbsp;
being said though, you can bet that if the layoffs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:58.440
work and the company starts doing well again,&nbsp;
the CEO is gonna say something like “You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:04.020
it wasn’t easy but I was willing to make&nbsp;
the hard decisions when it mattered, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:09.660
that’s why the company is where it’s at today.”&nbsp;
Conversely, if the recession passes by quickly and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:14.220
the layoffs turn out to be unnecessary and they&nbsp;
put the company a step behind the competition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:19.680
the CEO can shift all the blame to the&nbsp;
consultants. They can something like “You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:25.500
I never felt good about the layoffs. That’s why I&nbsp;
brought in McKinsey and paid them $30 million to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:31.380
do a thorough analysis of the business and the&nbsp;
industry. Unfortunately, it doesn’t look like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:36.480
they did their job correctly.” As you can see,&nbsp;
management consultants are the perfect scapegoat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:41.880
when it comes to justifying hard decisions and&nbsp;
blaming if the decisions don’t quite work out.

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:48.240
DEADWEIGHT:
In the end, objectively speaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:55.560
management consultants are indeed overpaid dead&nbsp;
weight. 100% of the time, they’re gonna be less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.560 --> 00:10:00.600
experienced than you, they’re gonna know less&nbsp;
about your business, and they’re gonna come to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:06.300
the same conclusions. And even if they don’t,&nbsp;
well it’s not like you’re gonna listen to them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:13.260
anyway. So, managements consultants rarely ever&nbsp;
provide any tangible value. And in an ideal world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:19.620
they would be completely pointless, but we don’t&nbsp;
live in a perfect world. In the real world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:26.040
companies are filled with politics, bureaucracy,&nbsp;
drama, ego, and leaders want to deflect as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:31.980
responsibility as possible. And it turns out that&nbsp;
management consultants are the perfect scapegoat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:36.720
when it comes to dealing with all of these&nbsp;
corporate inefficiencies. Now, I know that we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:42.840
been trashing on consultants this entire video,&nbsp;
but it’s not all bad. In fact, from a career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:48.960
perspective, it’s actually quite appealing. For&nbsp;
starters, you don’t have any technical skills. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:55.380
don’t need to know C++ or Java or the intricacies&nbsp;
of running servers of networks. You simply need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.380 --> 00:11:00.720
have strong communication and people skills. And&nbsp;
while this doesn’t come naturally to all of us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:06.540
for most people looking into consulting, it&nbsp;
does. Aside from not requiring any hard skills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:12.480
consulting jobs often come with a lot perks for&nbsp;
people who like to explore and experience. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:19.080
example, you wont be the same thing over and over&nbsp;
again. With each project, you’ll be working with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:24.180
new companies, traveling to new locations, and&nbsp;
consulting on different issues. To make things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:30.060
even better, consulting firms often rank extremely&nbsp;
favorably when it comes to company culture. BCG,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:35.400
for example, literally ranks number 1 when it&nbsp;
comes to employee approval of the CEO. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:39.960
let’s not forget about the compensation.&nbsp;
Not only are you paid super well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:45.240
but you’re entire compensation is in the&nbsp;
form of cash and bonus. So, for the better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:51.660
or for the worse, you don’t have to deal with&nbsp;
stock compensation. With all that being said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:57.780
like any job, this job has it’s cons as well.&nbsp;
You’ll be working long hours, you’ll rarely be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.780 --> 00:12:02.580
at home, you’ll always be on call, and at&nbsp;
the end of your career, you’ll have little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:09.300
to quantifiably show for you’re work. I mean,&nbsp;
its in the name of the job. You don’t manage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:16.080
you don’t lead, you don’t engineer, you don’t&nbsp;
create, and you don’t produce change. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:21.420
you simply consult. If you’re an impact driven&nbsp;
person who wants to produce quantifiable change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.420 --> 00:12:26.940
throughout your career, well, management&nbsp;
consulting is probably not for you. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:31.200
if you see your job as just a means&nbsp;
to an end and as a source of income,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:36.660
management consulting may be a great choice&nbsp;
for you. You’ll get to make a bunch of money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:42.720
meet a bunch of people, and simply experience&nbsp;
the world. But just keep in mind that these are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:48.060
the real reasons that companies actually hire you.&nbsp;
Would you become a management consultant? Comment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.060 --> 00:12:53.520
that down below. Also, drop a like if this video&nbsp;
gave you a better idea as to why companies pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:58.440
consultants so much. And of course, consider&nbsp;
checking out our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.440 --> 00:13:02.220
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
to see more questions logically answered.

